WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.443 --> 00:00:05.208
A question I often get: does the order of

2
00:00:05.208 --> 00:00:09.972
articles matter for data minimisation?

3
00:00:09.972 --> 00:00:14.056
With this person under 18 we see early on

4
00:00:14.056 --> 00:00:18.441
that the person is 17, and so we
don't need to look at the rest of the

5
00:00:18.441 --> 00:00:21.744
machine-executable laws and regulations.

6
00:00:21.744 --> 00:00:23.345
But isn't that because

7
00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:28.467
age is one of the first articles?

8
00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:31.987
Had it come later, would we still
have looked at those sensitive

9
00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:32.788
data points?

10
00:00:32.788 --> 00:00:34.868
The answer is yes, that's right.

11
00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:38.809
In this case, certainly.

12
00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:41.670
We discuss this with lawyers.

13
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.531
May you change the order
of laws and regulations?

14
00:00:45.531 --> 00:00:52.012
May you choose which
articles to run first?

15
00:00:52.012 --> 00:00:54.613
Lawyers disagree on that.

16
00:00:54.613 --> 00:00:57.934
For some laws and regulations
it makes no difference

17
00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:00.235
in which order you run the articles.

18
00:01:00.235 --> 00:01:02.816
For other laws and regulations it may.

19
00:01:02.816 --> 00:01:07.198
Does a later article
assume you have already

20
00:01:07.198 --> 00:01:08.415
read an earlier one?

21
00:01:08.415 --> 00:01:14.099
If there is room to choose the order,

22
00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:18.622
you may be able to minimise this.

23
00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:22.564
So that as few data points
as possible are touched,

24
00:01:22.564 --> 00:01:25.326
or as few sensitive data
points as possible.

25
00:01:25.326 --> 00:01:29.549
Perhaps we could even let the GDPR

26
00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:32.431
determine that order.

27
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:40.320
That law has a kind of meta-effect:
it says what is sensitive

28
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.225
and what you may and may not touch.
